p=doc.add_paragraph(); p.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER; p.paragraph_format.space_before=Pt(110); p.add_run('Project author: ____________________\nCourse / institution: ____________________')

page('Contents',['This report follows the supplied Project Report Submission Template and describes the objective, source data, recommendation method, application, limitations, and next steps. The source dataset has been downloaded and the TF-IDF index has been fitted.','Sections: Executive summary; project brief; problem and scope; questions and deliverables; dataset and fields; data quality; exploratory analysis; text and feature design; methodology; algorithm choices; evaluation plan; application design; setup; risks and limitations; milestones; individual details and references.'])
page('Executive Summary',['This project recommends restaurants similar to a restaurant name entered by the user. It represents restaurant review language with TF-IDF, compares those vectors with cosine similarity, filters for meaningful similarity, and sorts the remaining candidates by restaurant rating. Cuisine and neighbourhood text provide context when reviews are sparse.','The deliverable is a local Streamlit interface plus reproducible data download and model-building scripts. The source notebook points to the historical Zomato Bengaluru restaurant dataset. The fitted index contains 8,739 normalized restaurant profiles from 51,717 source rows, including 8,334 with review text and 6,618 with ratings. These are data coverage counts, not recommendation quality scores.','This is an educational content based prototype. It does not personalize from user behaviour, predict ratings, verify current operating status, or establish that similar review language means equivalent dining quality.'])
page('Project Brief',['Recommendation systems reduce the effort needed to find relevant items in large catalogs. A restaurant name is a useful seed item when a user already knows one place they like. Content based filtering can recommend other items whose descriptions resemble that seed.','The task focuses on review text, as requested, with cuisine and neighbourhood fields as supporting content. Ratings determine the final sort order. The user can select a restaurant, set the number of results, and adjust the minimum similarity threshold.'])
page('Problem, Scope, and Questions',['Input: one restaurant name selected from the data. Output: a ranked list of other restaurants with a similarity score, rating, vote count, cuisine, neighbourhood, and approximate cost when available.','In scope: download and inspect the source CSV, clean and aggregate restaurant records, extract review language, build an unsupervised text representation, retrieve similar restaurants, rank by rating, and present results in Streamlit. Out of scope: collaborative filtering, user profiles, live Zomato data, recommendation explanations based on causal claims, and production availability guarantees.','Guiding questions: Can review language distinguish restaurant profiles? Do cuisine and neighbourhood fields improve coverage when reviews are short? Does the rating-first ranking produce useful candidates among restaurants that pass a similarity threshold? How sensitive are recommendations to sparse and noisy reviews?'])
page('Dataset and Fields',['The reference notebook is `https://www.kaggle.com/code/chirag9073/zomato-recommendation-system`. Its referenced source is the Kaggle Zomato Bangalore Restaurants dataset, `himanshupoddar/zomato-bangalore-restaurants`. The downloaded CSV contains 51,717 source rows. After normalization and text filtering, the index contains 8,739 restaurants, 8,334 with reviews, 6,618 with parsed ratings, and a 60,000 term vocabulary. The historical snapshot describes Bengaluru venues and includes restaurant metadata and serialized review lists.','The downloader stores the source as `data/zomato.csv`. The full source file is stored at `data/zomato.csv`; the summary is reproducible with `python src/build_model.py`. These counts describe coverage only and do not establish recommendation relevance.'],(['Field','Purpose'],[['name','Restaurant identity and user search'],['reviews_list','Review text used for NLP similarity'],['rate','Restaurant rating used for ranking'],['votes','Rating support and tie breaking'],['cuisines','Supporting content for sparse reviews'],['location / listed_in(city)','Neighbourhood context'],['approx_cost(for two people)','Display context only']]))
page('Data Quality and Preparation',['The CSV may contain duplicate rows because restaurants appear under multiple listing categories. The pipeline groups records by normalized name, joins text fields, combines numeric ratings using vote counts as weights, and sums votes. This is a practical identity heuristic: similarly named branches may be merged, while spelling variants may remain separate.','The review field is often represented as a string containing tuples. The parser uses `ast.literal_eval` rather than executing arbitrary code. If parsing fails, it retains the field as text. Missing reviews become empty strings; missing ratings stay missing and cannot be recommended.','After downloading, inspect row count, duplicate names, blank review share, rating parsing, category variants, and extreme review lengths. The project summary reports basic final counts and vocabulary size. A manual sample audit is recommended before interpreting results.'])
page('Exploratory Analysis Plan',['Before fitting, examine missing values by field, restaurant counts by neighbourhood and cuisine, review length distribution, rating distribution, vote skew, repeated names, and review volume. These checks help detect weak coverage and dominant high-frequency terms.','Review texts can contain noise, templated phrases, inconsistent spelling, mixed languages, and rating markers. TF-IDF limits the effect of common words but does not remove sarcasm, negation errors, spam, or platform-specific bias. The historic collection date also means current relevance cannot be inferred.'])
page('Text and Feature Design',['For each normalized restaurant, review text is concatenated with cuisine and neighbourhood descriptions. TF-IDF uses lowercasing, accent stripping, English stop words, unigrams and bigrams, sublinear term frequency, a bounded vocabulary, and document frequency limits. The resulting sparse vector gives higher weight to terms that distinguish restaurants from the broader collection.','Cosine similarity measures the angle between two nonzero vectors and ranges from zero to one for nonnegative TF-IDF. It is used for candidate similarity, not as a rating estimate. Short documents can have unstable profiles; supporting metadata helps, but may also make recommendations reflect cuisine or location rather than reviews alone.'])
page('Methodology and Algorithms',['This is an unsupervised, content based retrieval task. No target labels are learned and no user-item interaction matrix is available. TF-IDF is the language representation; cosine similarity is the nearest-neighbour measure.','For a selected restaurant, the system computes similarity against all restaurant vectors, removes the source item, filters candidates below a configurable threshold, and excludes candidates without a parsed rating. It orders remaining candidates by rating descending, then similarity and vote count. The rating is a source field, not an output predicted by the model.','The app displays the similarity value so users can distinguish stronger text matches. A higher rated candidate may appear above a more similar candidate because the requested final ordering prioritizes rating.'])
page('Evaluation Plan and Results',['Offline evaluation should be completed after the data download. Separate held out review text or use a leave-one-review-out setup to avoid comparing a restaurant profile with the same text used to construct it. Candidate relevance can be judged with Precision@K, Recall@K, NDCG@K, and human review of examples. Rating sorted results should also be checked against similarity-first results.','No numeric quality score is reported because this unsupervised project does not yet have a relevance-labelled test set. The fitted artifact and `reports/model_summary.json` document the data coverage and representation size. A future evaluation should use held out reviews, add reproducible ranking metrics, and include human-reviewed examples.'])
page('Streamlit Application Design',['The app provides a restaurant search, recommendation count, similarity threshold, ranked results table, and expandable explanation of the pipeline. Summary metrics show restaurants indexed, those with ratings, and those containing review text.','If the data file is absent, the page shows setup commands and links to the Kaggle notebook and dataset. If the data schema cannot be recognized, it gives a concise error and directs the user to check expected restaurant and review fields. The model index is cached during the session to avoid repeated vectorization.'])
page('Installation and Operation',['From the project folder, create a virtual environment and install `requirements.txt`. Configure the Kaggle API token in the standard user configuration location, then run `python src/download_dataset.py`, `python src/build_model.py`, and `streamlit run app.py`.','The downloader fetches the public Kaggle dataset by slug and extracts `zomato.csv`. The builder writes `models/zomato_tfidf.joblib` and `reports/model_summary.json`. The joblib artifact is version sensitive; regenerate it after changing the source data or scikit-learn version. Keep Kaggle credentials outside the project and source control.'])
page('Risks and Limitations',['The dataset is historical and limited to Bengaluru. The venues, reviews, ratings, and costs may have changed. The project has no live service integration and cannot establish present availability or price.','Restaurant names are imperfect identifiers. Branches can be collapsed by name grouping. Review text may be sparse, biased, multilingual, or influenced by promotions. Similarity can favour generic wording; ratings can be unstable for venues with few votes. The app exposes vote count and similarity as context but does not make a confidence guarantee.','Do not use recommendations as a safety, health, accessibility, or dietary assurance. Users should independently verify current details and allergen information.'])
page('Milestones and Resources',['Completed in this workspace: source dataset download, project scaffold, review parser, TF-IDF index, rating-first ranking, Streamlit interface, model summary, README, and report. Remaining work is to inspect recommendation examples and complete relevance-based evaluation.'],(['Milestone','Status','Evidence / next action'],[['Implement content based NLP','Complete','TF-IDF and cosine engine'],['Create Streamlit app','Complete','app.py'],['Prepare source dataset','Complete','data/zomato.csv'],['Fit and summarize index','Complete','Model artifact and summary JSON'],['Review recommendation quality','Planned','Add labels, ranking metrics, and human review']]))
page('Individual Details, References, and Glossary',['Complete the submission fields before handing in: Name: ____________________; Email: ____________________; Phone: ____________________; Course / institution: ____________________.','References: Chirag9073, Zomato Recommendation System notebook, Kaggle, https://www.kaggle.com/code/chirag9073/zomato-recommendation-system. Dataset contributor: Himanshu Poddar, Zomato Bangalore Restaurants, Kaggle, https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/himanshupoddar/zomato-bangalore-restaurants.','Glossary: content based recommendation compares item attributes; NLP means natural language processing; TF-IDF means term frequency-inverse document frequency; cosine similarity compares vector direction; unsupervised learning discovers structure without target labels; Precision@K is the fraction of the first K results judged relevant; NDCG@K rewards relevant results appearing earlier.'])
